"At Princeton he wrote an intensely religious senior thesis and thought of becoming an Episcopal priest. He graduated in 1943 and enlisted in the army. He served as an infantryman in New Guinea, where he was awarded the Bronze Star, and in the Philippines, where he saw heavy fighting. It was there that he lost his Christian faith. After Japan surrendered he was part of the occupying army and saw what was left of Hiroshima. He was promoted to sergeant. Then he refused an order to discipline a fellow soldier, because he believed that no punishment was justified, and he was demoted to private. He left the army in January 1946.": "7bde6279107c",
"Fame did not change his habits. He gave almost no interviews and described himself as having a horror of the limelight. Students remember a teacher who took careful notes on what they said and presented the philosophers he lectured on in their strongest form. In 1989 student protesters in Tiananmen Square held up copies of his book. In 1999 he received the National Humanities Medal. A series of strokes from 1995 slowed his work, and he died at home in Lexington, Massachusetts, in 2002, aged eighty-one.": "9f575a86176b",
"From that position, he argued, two principles would be chosen, and in a fixed order. First, equal basic liberties for all, which may not be traded for economic gain. Second, fair equality of opportunity, and inequalities only where they benefit the least advantaged. He did not claim to have deduced these from nothing. His method, which he called reflective equilibrium, is to move back and forth between principles and our firmest particular judgments, such as that slavery is unjust, adjusting each to the other until they fit. Behind the principles is a view of society as a fair system of cooperation over generations, in which the lucky and the unlucky share one anothers fate.": "30c757e29115",
"He taught at Cornell and at MIT, and in 1962 moved to Harvard, where he remained until he retired. In 1958 he had published a paper called Justice as Fairness. He spent the next thirteen years turning it into a book, circulating drafts among students and colleagues and rewriting in response. A Theory of Justice appeared in 1971. Its preface begins, This is a long book, not only in pages. At the time many philosophers believed that their subject could say nothing substantive about how society should be arranged. The book changed that. Nozick, who disagreed with nearly all of it, wrote that political philosophers must now either work within Rawlss theory or explain why not.": "3cd2a113827a",
"He went back to Princeton for a doctorate in moral philosophy, and married Margaret Fox in 1949. They had four children. A year at Oxford in 1952 brought him into contact with Isaiah Berlin and the legal philosopher H. L. A. Hart. Fifty years after the bombing, in one of the few pieces he wrote for a general audience, he argued that the destruction of Hiroshima had been a grave wrong.": "a32c6f957a0f",
"His biographer Thomas Pogge calls the loss of the brothers the most important events of his childhood. It is hard not to connect them with the idea that runs through his work: that who lives and who dies, who is gifted and who is not, who is born into which house, are matters of luck for which no one can take credit or blame. Rawls rarely spoke of it.": "750b5189316c",
"John Rawls was born in Baltimore in 1921. His father was a prominent lawyer and his mother campaigned for womens right to vote. In 1928, when he was seven, he caught diphtheria. His brother Bobby, twenty months younger, came into his room to keep him company, caught the disease, and died. The following winter Rawls had pneumonia. Another younger brother, Tommy, caught it from him and also died. Rawls recovered both times. He developed a stutter that stayed with him for life, and he himself connected it with these deaths.": "69d2f6c51d65",
"Rawls begins from a conviction: Each person possesses an inviolability founded on justice that even the welfare of society as a whole cannot override. Utilitarianism, the leading theory of his day, could not explain this. If only the total of happiness matters, the suffering of a few may be outweighed by the gains of many. Rawls wanted a theory in which each person counts separately. He found it in the old idea of the social contract, which he made more abstract. Principles are just if free and equal people would agree to them in a fair situation. He described that situation as an original position in which the parties stand behind a veil of ignorance: no one knows his place in society, his class position or social status; nor does he know his fortune in the distribution of natural assets and abilities.": "f63552f51868",
"Rawls refused. In The Law of Peoples he argued that justice as fairness is a theory for a single society whose members share institutions and take part in governing them. Between peoples he proposed a shorter list of duties: to respect human rights, to refrain from aggressive war, and to assist societies burdened by unfavorable conditions until they can manage their own affairs. He set no continuing duty to reduce inequality between nations. Whether this was sound realism about what distant peoples can owe one another, or a failure to follow his own argument, is still disputed (Big Questions A24).": "a2dcdb98942c",
"Rawls took the point seriously enough to write a second book. In Political Liberalism he no longer presented justice as fairness as the truth about morality. He presented it as a basis on which citizens who disagree deeply about religion and the good can still live together on terms each can accept from within his own tradition. He called this an overlapping consensus. Critics on the other side, such as G. A. Cohen, thought he had conceded too much, and that a theory of justice should say what is right whether or not everyone can be brought to accept it.": "6b20c4f6020a",
"The objection that troubled Rawls most came from critics such as Michael Sandel, who are often called communitarians. The parties behind the veil know nothing of their religion, their family, or their idea of a good life. Sandel argued that such a chooser is no one in particular, and that the choice is rigged from the start in favor of a liberal view on which people are, at bottom, independent choosers and their attachments are optional. For most people, their faith or their duty to their parents is part of who they are. Asking them to set it aside when they reason about justice, he said, is taking a side.": "c8fccac2078f",
"The veil hides a persons class and talents. Many readers expected that it would also hide his nationality. Being born in a poor country is as much a matter of luck as being born into a poor family, and it affects a life far more. Philosophers who had learned from Rawls, among them Thomas Pogge and Charles Beitz, drew what seemed the obvious conclusion: the difference principle should apply to the whole world.": "172ce13f4beb",
"The veil of ignorance is an exercise in thinking without I and mine. For the length of one argument a person sets aside his name, his family, his advantages, and his interests, and asks what would be good from no position in particular. Buddhist training aims at something similar as a lasting change. The meditations on loving-kindness extend to all beings the wish one naturally has for oneself, and the teaching of non-self holds that the line we draw around my welfare has no ultimate basis. Śāntidevas argument that suffering should be removed simply because it is suffering, whoevers it is, would be at home behind the veil (Dharma and Western Philosophy D19).": "a65c913c5161",
"They differ on luck. For Rawls, where and to whom a person is born is arbitrary, and justice is largely the work of correcting for it. In the Buddhist account birth is connected with past action, and so it is not arbitrary. The two views come apart less in practice than one might expect. The Buddha denied that birth fixes a persons worth: Youre not a lowlife by birth, nor by birth are you a brahmin. Youre a lowlife by your deeds, by deeds youre a brahmin. He admitted people of every class to his community on equal terms. And the tradition does not treat a persons past as a reason for others to withhold help. They differ more deeply in aim. Rawls asks what institutions free and equal citizens could accept. The Buddha asks how a person comes to stop grasping at a share at all.": "c9f6cb013d50",
